Luxury Design System
Background Color: #12110F (Warm Obsidian). It’s softer and richer than pure black.

Primary Text Color: #ECE8E1 (Alabaster). Easy on the eyes, creates an elegant contrast.

Accent Color: #A99260 (Tarnished Gold). Use this only for hover states, small subheadings, and thin structural lines.

Heading Font: Cormorant Garamond (Google Font). Use this for large display text. It has beautiful, sharp serifs that instantly read as high-end editorial.

Body/Utility Font: Manrope or Inter (Google Font). Use this for paragraphs, tech stack lists, and navigation. Keep it light (font-weight: 300 or 400).

The Architecture & Section Prompts
Here is the page structure. You missed one crucial section for a luxury site: The Philosophy. High-end brands always tell you how they think before they show you what they do.

Feed these exact prompts into your code editor or AI assistant section by section.

1. The Navigation Bar (Minimalist & Fixed)
Prompt:

"Build a fixed top navigation bar using Next.js and Tailwind CSS. The background should be #12110F with an opacity of 90% and a backdrop blur of sm. Add a 1px solid bottom border using color #2A2826.

Layout: Flexbox, justify-between, items-center, py-6, px-12.
Left Side: The name 'DAWINDER SINGH' in Manrope, uppercase, tracking-widest (tracking-[0.2em]), text size text-sm, color #ECE8E1.
Right Side: A simple flex row with links: 'Expertise', 'Selected Works', 'Contact'. Font is Manrope, text-xs, uppercase, tracking-wider.
Interaction: On hover, the links should transition smoothly to the accent color #A99260 over 300ms. No background hover effects, just the text color changing."

2. The Hero Section (The Hook)
Prompt:

"Create a full-screen hero section (min-h-screen) with a grid layout. The background is #12110F.

Layout: A 12-column grid (grid-cols-12). gap-8, px-12, items-center.
Left Column (Spans 5 cols): An image container. The image must have a stark black-and-white filter (grayscale contrast-125). The container should be tall and portrait-oriented (aspect ratio 3:4), with sharp, unrounded corners (rounded-none). No borders.
Right Column (Spans 7 cols):

Pre-heading: text-xs, uppercase, tracking-widest, color #A99260, font Manrope. Text: '01 // CREATIVE DEVELOPER'. Margin bottom mb-4.

Main Heading: Font Cormorant Garamond, text-8xl or text-9xl, tight leading (leading-none), color #ECE8E1. Text: 'Engineering \n Digital \n Elegance.' The word 'Digital' should be italicized.

Subtext: Font Manrope, text-lg, font-light, max-w-md, color #A39F98, mt-8. Text: 'Architecting high-performance web applications with a focus on precision, speed, and immaculate design.'
GSAP Animation: On page load, the image should unmask from bottom to top over 1.5 seconds (ease: "power4.out"). The heading text should use a SplitText (or split by words) and reveal upwards with a slight stagger and fade-in."

3. The Philosophy (The Missing Page/Section)
Prompt:

"Create a philosophy section. Background #12110F, py-32, px-12.
Layout: Flex column, centered.
Content: A massive blockquote. Font Cormorant Garamond, text-5xl to text-6xl, text-center, max-width max-w-4xl, color #ECE8E1, leading relaxed.
Quote: 'Good code is invisible. Great design is felt. I build systems that exist seamlessly at the intersection of both.'
GSAP Animation: As this section enters the viewport, use ScrollTrigger to slowly fade it in and translate it on the Y-axis by -30px. The movement should be incredibly slow and elegant, tied to the user's scroll speed (scrub: 1)."

4. Technologies (Editorial Layout)
Prompt:

"Build a technologies section. No grid of colorful logos—that looks cheap. Background #12110F, py-24, px-12.
Header: Top border 1px solid #2A2826. '02 // EXPERTISE' in Manrope, text-xs, uppercase, tracking-widest, color #A99260, pt-4, mb-16.
Layout: A two-column layout (md:grid md:grid-cols-2 md:gap-16).
Left side: A brief paragraph in Manrope, text-xl, font-light, color #ECE8E1, detailing a focus on modern, scalable architecture.
Right side: A list of technologies (Next.js, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, GSAP, React).
Styling the list: Each technology is a flex row, justify-between, items-center, py-6. Border bottom 1px solid #2A2826. Font Cormorant Garamond, text-4xl, color #ECE8E1.
Hover Effect: When hovering over a tech item, the text color changes to #A99260, and it slightly indents to the right by 10px (translate-x-2 transition-transform duration-500)."

5. Selected Works (The Projects)
Prompt:

"Create a projects section. Background #12110F, py-32, px-12.
Header: Top border 1px solid #2A2826. '03 // SELECTED WORKS' in Manrope, text-xs, uppercase, tracking-widest, color #A99260, pt-4, mb-16.
Layout: Vertical stacking cards. Use a flex column with a gap-32.
Individual Project Layout: Each project is a full-width container.

Image Container: Takes up 100% width, tall aspect ratio (aspect-[16/9] or aspect-[21/9]). The image should have a subtle parallax effect inside the container using GSAP.

Project Info (Below Image): Flexbox justify-between, items-start, mt-6.

Left Info: Project title (e.g., 'Kootenay Fresh', 'Barber SaaS') in Cormorant Garamond, text-5xl. Below it, a short description in Manrope, text-sm, color #A39F98, mt-2.

Right Info: Tech stack used (e.g., 'Next.js // Supabase // Tailwind') in Manrope, text-xs, uppercase, tracking-widest.
Projects to Include: 1. Luxury Cleaning Service Estimator, 2. Barber Booking SaaS, 3. Dynamic QR Code Generator.
GSAP Animation: Implement a ScrollTrigger parallax effect where the images move slightly slower than the scroll bar to create depth."

6. The Footer (Contact & Impact)
Prompt:

"Build the footer. Background #0A0908 (slightly darker than the main background for contrast). py-32, px-12.
Layout: Centered flex column.
Main Call to Action: Massive text. Font Cormorant Garamond, text-[10vw] (scales with viewport), leading-none, color #ECE8E1, text 'LET'S TALK'. Wrap this in an anchor tag that opens the default email client.
Hover Effect on Text: On hover, the text should italicize or shift to the accent color #A99260.
Bottom Bar: mt-24, flex justify-between, items-center, border top 1px solid #2A2826, pt-8.

Left: 'Ontario, Canada' Manrope, text-xs, tracking-widest.

Right: Links to 'LinkedIn', 'GitHub', 'Email'. Manrope, text-xs, uppercase, hover to #A99260."

The Typographic Hierarchy
Luxury design relies on extreme contrast between your headings and your body text. You want a dramatic, high-fashion Serif paired with an invisible, highly legible Sans-Serif.

The Display Font (Headings): Cormorant Garamond (or Playfair Display).

Psychology: Serifs with high contrast (thick and thin strokes) whisper "heritage, bespoke, and expensive."

Usage: Use this only for your massive hero text (text-7xl to text-9xl), project titles, and large quotes.

Weights to use: Regular (400) or Light (300). Never use bold; it looks clunky. Use italics selectively for specific words to add an editorial flair (e.g., "Engineering Digital Elegance").

The Utility Font (Paragraphs, Navbar, Tech Stack): Manrope (or Neue Montreal if you can get it, otherwise Inter with custom tracking).

Psychology: A geometric sans-serif implies modern precision, logic, and engineering—perfect for a developer. It gets out of the way so the Serif can shine.

Usage: Navbar links, small over-titles ("01 // EXPERTISE"), paragraphs, and footer details.

Weights to use: Light (300) or Regular (400). Always add tracking (letter-spacing) to uppercase utility text (e.g., tracking-widest) to make it breathe.

2. The Tonal Color Palette
This palette uses subtle shifts in deep, warm tones to create depth without breaking the user's immersion.

Primary Background (#12110F - Warm Obsidian):

Where to use: The main <body> background, your Hero section, and the whitespace between sections.

Secondary Background / Elevation (#1C1A18 - Smoked Umber):

Where to use: Use this for the background of your "Selected Works" project cards or to highlight a specific block of text. Because it is just a fraction lighter than the main background, it creates a subtle physical "lift" (elevation) without feeling like a totally different color block.

The Navbar (#12110F at 80% Opacity + Blur):

Where to use: The fixed navigation bar.

The Strategy: Do not make the navbar a different solid color. Instead, use your Primary Background color, drop the opacity to 80%, and add a CSS backdrop blur (backdrop-blur-md in Tailwind). Add a 1px bottom border of #2A2826. As the user scrolls, the page content will "ghost" beautifully underneath the navbar. It stays readable but feels deeply integrated.

Primary Text (#EBE5D9 - Cashmere White):

Where to use: Headings and primary titles. It’s not stark white, so it won’t burn the retinas, but it’s bright enough to demand attention.

Secondary Text (#9E988F - Faded Linen):

Where to use: Paragraphs, descriptions, and footer text. Dimming the body text ensures the reader's eye is drawn directly to your headings and project images first.

The Highlight / Action Color (#CBA365 - Aged Brass):

Where to use: Hover states on your navbar links, the tiny "01 //", "02 //" section markers, and the text for your main call-to-action button.

Psychology: Gold/Brass against dark warm gray is the ultimate signifier of premium quality (think luxury hotel branding or high-end watches). It guides the eye exactly where you want the user to click.